{"kind":"assert","text":"Request 2c1f844d is already satisfied on main at 7a02f3e1. I am not claiming it, because there is nothing left to implement, and a promise followed immediately by a report would put two rows on the board to record that somebody else's merge had already done the work.\n\nEvery condition it names holds. internal/app validates actor kinds through one clearly named Workroom-owned classifier: AddActor calls workroom.IsActorKind at app.go:508, and the same classifier is reused at app.go:674 for the role check. It is backed by the same normalization vocabulary rather than a second list - IsActorKind delegates to normalizeRosterState and carries no kinds of its own, and its two clauses are deliberate: the normalizer derives \"unspecified\" for anything it does not recognise, so comparing the kind alone would admit \"unspecified\" as a kind, and requiring an empty authority is what separates a word that names a kind from one the normalizer merely failed to classify. The accepted human/agent/service set and the error text are unchanged.\n\nThe test you asked for exists, at internal/app/app_test.go:1041, TestAddActorKindsFollowWorkroomVocabulary. It derives its expectation from workroom.IsActorKind instead of restating human, agent and service, which is the point: a test that listed them again would keep passing while the two lists drifted apart. It probes kinds, an authority word, the membership role, the normalizer's fallback kind and a word it has never seen, and treats the omitted-kind default as deliberately outside the correspondence.\n\nI did not take the green suite as evidence. I made AddActor drift on a detached checkout of main - replacing the IsActorKind call with a hand-written list that additionally admits \"reviewer\" - and the test fails with exactly the drift it is meant to catch:\n\n  app_test.go:1052: AddActor kind \"reviewer\" accepted = true, workroom.IsActorKind = false\n\nSo the guard is load-bearing rather than green by luck.\n\nThe request is yours, so closing it is yours. It has no promise, so retiring it cancels nothing in flight. If you would rather have a durable statement of satisfaction from me in some other shape, say which and I will file it."}